<h3>Method 1 — two command lines (recommended)</h3>
<pre><code><span class="c"># 1) Add the harness marketplace</span>
/plugin marketplace add lev-factory/harness
<span class="c"># 2) Install the harness plugin</span>
/plugin install harness</code></pre>
<p>When the install screen appears, install with <strong>User scope</strong> (used across my whole account). If you want to use it only in a specific project, choose project scope.</p>

<h3>Method 2 — from the /plugin screen</h3>
<ol>
    <li>Enter <code>/plugin</code> → move to the <strong>Marketplace</strong> tab with the <strong>right arrow key</strong>.</li>
    <li>Enter <code>lev-factory/harness</code> in <strong>Add Marketplace</strong> → Enter.</li>
    <li>Select <strong>harness</strong> from the list → Enter on the install screen → install with <strong>User scope</strong>.</li>
</ol>

<h3>After installing — reload and verify</h3>
<pre><code><span class="c"># Apply the plugin to the current session</span>
/reload
<span class="c"># If the following autocompletes/runs, the install succeeded</span>
/harness</code></pre>
<p>If autocomplete pops up when you type <code>/harness</code>, you are ready. Now move on to <a href="#!hnp-first-run">First harness run</a>.</p>

<div class="note warn">
    <span class="nh">Caution — check the marketplace address</span>
    The <code>lev-factory/harness</code> above is <strong>an example value mentioned in the lecture</strong>. For an actual install, check the publisher's <strong>GitHub repository path</strong> and enter that (search term: <strong>"하네스 100"</strong>). If the install does not work, try alternating between <strong>the command approach ↔ the /plugin screen approach</strong>.
</div>

<div class="note">
    <span class="nh">Tip for in-house environments</span>
    In an in-house network or proxy environment, GitHub access may be restricted. If adding the marketplace fails, first check the <strong>network/proxy policy</strong> and <strong>Git access permissions</strong>. The plugin must be <strong>installed separately on each machine</strong> (agent definitions can be shared via Git, but the plugin itself is a personal install).
</div>
